    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90453.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 117,
    "turn_text": "I can remember a grandpa. For Rahm Emanuel's seat. Sure. I remember November of 2008 standing next to the stage when the president to be walked out with his family and promised them that puppy. And my wife said, so what are you going to do? There's a race with 23 people. I was fortunate to win a special election. And to get to your question, you get sworn in by yourself. They literally the next day they vote after the general. You go to Washington DC, they take a vote, they stop, Nancy Pelosi swore me in, and then you look up and you go, what the heck is House Resolution 2342? So unlike most of the who get elected the first time, they go to Harvard and they get a class on this and they get accustomed to it. You're thrown into it right away. That's part of it. I think the thing that struck me first that I had to learn is maybe a lesson for today. Congressman Mark Kirk zet down next to me after I was sworn in and he said, most of the time anything that gets done here gets done in the middle. which was, I think, very true for a lot of our legislation. I think a lot of us would have wanted to go farther when we passed the health care law, for example, but it was an amazing leap forward for health care for Americans",
